g the war  efforts to saw its source at the  energies and minds of  analyst that the suggestion was made  that the problem be drops over Germany  as the ultimate instrument of  intellectual sabotage and so you can  imagine how I feel so there's there's  lots of approximate solution methods  that do very well and various kinds of  reward distributions and that are used  in practice successfully and you can  think of a/b testing as one of them in  the multi unbanded literature is  something that's called this epsilon  first strategy where you have a certain  time period where you first explore all  the possible options uniformly at random  to gather evidence and then at some  point you decide okay this is my best  possible choice I will forever exploit  this one and that's basically what I'd  be testing does slightly more  sophisticated choice you can have  epsilon greedy methods where you always  choose the best action at this  particular time but a small amount of  time epsilon you explore random of  opportunities so this make sure that you  don't get stuck you know in a suboptimal  arm slightly better so there's some some  more advanced techniques by the way all  these are very easily implementable in  Python or in any language upper  confidence bound it's also called  optimism in the face of uncertainty  basically it says oK we've haven't  explored these arms quite sufficiently  yet so we don't know what our estimates  are so we might want to sample these a  bit more that kind of stuff  last one Thompson sampling is my  favorite one because it's Bayesian you  have a you start with a prior over the  arms and after each trial you get an  updated posterior and then you sample  from the posterior to pick your choices  it's quite nice work to in practice so  I'm not just making this up the a very  simple experiment if you have two arms  the newly distributed rewards so zeros  and ones one arm has 50% chance of  giving you one the other one has a 60%  chance so you can think of this as some  sort of conversion rate for example 50  percent that's quite high and 60 percent  is something you might not observe but  we do observe if we simulate it so you  can run experiments with different  strategies so for a/b testing I  mm trials until deciding I was going to  go with the with the winning one and I  got reasonably lucky to choose the  winning one but I left quite a lot of  conversion on the table and all the  other methods that I mentioned converge  much quicker through the optimal method  so there's lots of extensions you can do  these you can have a non-stationary  rewards contextual bandits is very  interesting because you might what you  might have some additional information  using features if you're serving  websites your your user features might  be important that's it  [Applause]

